{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/shell/store.cljc; sed -n '1,160p;210,260p;565,745p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/shell/dht.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"(ns yin.shell.store\n  \"The composition's store-selection factory\n   (docs/design/yin.repl.dht-composition.md sections 3 and 6).\n\n   The engine's own selection (`yin.repl.store`) knows two stores —\n   `mem` and `file:<dir>` — and answers a generic `dao.jing` content\n   handle for each.  Every storage implementation past those two is a\n   composition's: it owns the scheme, the checked spec, the open, and the\n   transport state the open produces.  This namespace is that factory for\n   the shell compositions, so a host entry point resolves one\n   `--index-store` value against one table instead of asking the engine\n   about a backend the engine must never name.\n\n   It owns no socket, atom, promise, callback, clock or namespace global:\n   selection is a pure dispatch, and the state an open produces is\n   answered as two separate values — the engine's handle and the\n   composition's node — never as one handle with a node inside it.\"\n  (:require [yin.repl.store :as store]\n            [yin.shell.dht :as shell.dht]\n            [yin.shell.query :as shell.query]\n            [yin.shell.session :as session]))\n\n\n(def dht-scheme\n  \"The store scheme the DHT composition registers with the engine's\n   parser, as plain data: a prefix to match, a label for the refusal and\n   help text, and a directory-to-spec projection.  The engine matches the\n   prefix and calls `:parse`; it never learns what the answer means.\"\n  {:prefix shell.dht/dht-scheme\n   :label (str shell.dht/dht-scheme \"<dir>\")\n   :type :dht\n   :parse (fn [dir] {:type :dht :dir dir})})\n\n\n(def schemes\n  \"Every scheme this factory adds to the engine's own two, in the order\n   the parser tries them.\"\n  [dht-scheme])\n\n\n(defn parse-arg\n  \"The `--index-store` value as a spec, over the engine's two schemes and\n   `schemes`: `:mem`, `{:type :file :dir dir}`, or `{:type :dht :dir\n   dir}`.  A missing value, an unknown scheme and an empty directory are\n   refused with every supported form named, so the refusal an operator\n   reads lists the composition's stores too.\"\n  [value]\n  (store/parse-arg value schemes))\n\n\n(defn composition-spec?\n  \"True when `spec` names a store a composition opens rather than one the\n   engine's own selection does.\"\n  [spec]\n  (boolean (and (map? spec)\n                (some #(= (:type %) (:type spec)) schemes))))\n\n\n(defn checked-spec\n  \"Validate an `:index-store-spec` over both selections: the engine's own\n   `:mem` and `{:type :file :dir dir}`, and each registered composition's\n   own checked spec.  Anything else is refused with its reason.\"\n  [spec]\n  (if (composition-spec? spec)\n    (shell.dht/checked-spec spec)\n    (store/checked-spec spec)))\n\n\n(defn open\n  \"Open the store `spec` names, answering the two values of section 3's\n   ownership split:\n\n   ```clojure\n   {:index-store <the generic dao.jing handle the engine is constructed\n                  with>\n    :node <the composition's transport step-owner value, or nil for a\n           store with no transport>\n    :recovery <what the open validated, or nil>\n    :publication-policy <the immutable generic policy of section 5, or nil>\n    :retain-index-on-reset? bool}\n   ```\n\n   `opts` reaches the composition that opens.  They are either its own\n   names (`yin.shell.dht/open`'s `:key`, `:ws`, `:write-heads!`,\n   `:open-store`) or the host driver's neutral ones (`:publisher-key`,\n   `:ws-host`), translated here, so an engine entry point never learns what\n   a composition calls its own inputs.  `:principals`, `:admission` and\n   `:name-env` join the prepared `:link-source` of `:engine-inputs`: the\n   further `yin.repl/create-state` options this composition injects, so a\n   `(reset)` or a `(vm ...)` rebuild reconstructs a session that still\n   resolves signed names and still serves the composition's modules.\n   `:session` is the transport half of the composition session\n   (`yin.shell.session/transport-state`), which a host carries beside its\n   engine value and never inside it.\n\n   A refusal releases whatever the failed open acquired, exactly once, and\n   propagates as itself.\n\n   The engine is handed `:index-store` and never `:node`; the composition\n   keeps the node and steps it\n   (docs/design/yin.repl.dht-composition.md sections 3 and 6).\"\n  ([spec] (open spec {}))\n  ([spec opts]\n   (if (composition-spec? spec)\n     (let [_ (when (or (:content-store opts) (:content-client opts))\n               (throw (ex-info \"A composed store cannot use a second content source\"\n                               {:index-store-spec spec})))\n           key (or (:key opts) (:publisher-key opts))\n           opened (shell.dht/open spec {:key key\n                                        :ws (or (:ws opts) (:ws-host opts))\n                                        :write-heads! (:write-heads! opts)\n                                        :open-store (:open-store opts)})]\n       (assoc opened\n              :engine-inputs\n              {:link-source (shell.dht/link-source\n                              spec\n                              {:key key\n                               :principals (:principals opts)\n                               :admission (get opts :admission {})\n                               :name-env (:name-env opts)})\n               :host-modules shell.query/registrations}\n              :session (session/transport-state (:node opened) key)))\n     (let [handle (store/open (store/checked-spec spec))]\n       {:index-store handle\n        :node nil\n        :recovery (:recovery handle)\n        :publication-policy (:publication-policy handle)\n        :retain-index-on-reset? (store/retain-on-reset? handle)\n        :engine-inputs nil\n        :session nil}))))\n\n\n(defn durable?\n  \"True when `store` is a durable directory store the engine's own\n   selection opened (yin.repl.store/durable?).\"\n  [store]\n  (store/durable? store))\n\n\n(defn retain-on-reset?\n  \"True when a session rebuild continues `store`'s published index rather\n   than starting an empty one: the store's declared policy, or the\n   durable directory's own fact when it declares none\n   (yin.repl.store/retain-on-reset?).\"\n  [store]\n  (store/retain-on-reset? store))\n\n\n(defn close!\n  \"Release `store`'s lifecycle resources.  Idempotent, and the one place a\n   composition's resource owner closes the store it opened\n   (docs/design/yin.repl.dht-composition.md sections 3 and 6).\"\n  [store]\n  (store/close! store))\n\n(ns yin.shell.dht\n  \"The DHT index-store composition, `--index-store dht:<dir>` (DHT epic\n   slice S5: docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md section 10; docs/design/\n   yin.repl.dao.space-index.md, \\\"DHT store\\\"), relocated out of the\n   engine tree by docs/design/yin.repl.dht-composition.md sections 2, 6\n   and 7.\n\n   This namespace is the storage implementation the engine never names.\n   It owns store selection and opening, HEAD persistence, announce,\n   signed-head deposit and following, hydration, transport stepping,\n   scheduling, banners, refusal rendering and shutdown — the whole of\n   section 3's \\\"Composition ownership\\\" row — and it crosses inward only\n   what section 4 allows: a generic `dao.jing` content handle, validated\n   recovery data, an immutable publication policy, and correlated generic\n   completions.\n\n   It is composed with the plain Clojure path, `dao.space.dht`, and adds\n   only what the shell owns: the durable directory, the lines it prints,\n   and when it admits evaluation.\n\n   `open` opens `<dir>` exactly as `file:<dir>` does: the exclusive lock,\n   the content log, HEAD, the validated snapshot (dao.space.store), and\n   joins a `dao.space.dht` node with that locked store as its `:local`.\n   The indexer publishes through the node's store: each put inserts\n   locally and appends one replicate request, and the manifest reads back\n   locally, so a round never waits on the network.  The HEAD write then\n   announces the publication to the node.  Section 3's ordering contract\n   is what that write preserves — materialize/read-back, then durable HEAD\n   success, then announce and deposit — and nothing here restates or\n   reorders it: `open` answers the engine's handle and the composition's\n   node as two separate values, so the engine is never handed something\n   whose transport state it could step.\n\n   `step` is the node's one step owner, called by the host's single\n   ticker (yin.shell.main/step-all) with the host's clock reading; it\n   renders the node's events as the lines the REPL prints: where the\n   socket bound, and for each publication its result (acknowledged,\n   sent to N peers, or partial or not acknowledged with the count of\n   blobs not sent, the first reason, and whether the node is retrying)\n   and each later change of it (yin.vm.linker.dht.md 5.5.6).  A load's\n   failure reason is data; it is rendered as text here.\n\n   A reader handed a manifest address (`:manifest`, a pin) loads it\n   through `dao.space.dht/load-index` before the shell admits any\n   evaluation, as a restart installs its recovery first, then writes it\n   to HEAD and rehydrates the indexer from the loaded datoms, so `q`\n   answers the remote run's facts.  A load that cannot complete, or a\n   socket that cannot bind, refuses the shell (`refusal`); it never\n   starts over an empty index.  Remote indexes are also reachable at the\n   prompt, through the `dao.space.dht` host module (yin.shell.query),\n   over the same node.  Hydration gates evaluation input\n   (`admitting?`); head following never does — following updates the\n   resolver, and only an explicit pinned hydration installs recovery\n   before evaluation is admitted (section 3).\n\n   The published head trace (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md,\n   slice H3) is composed here too, the shell owning the file, the lines\n   and the dial:\n   * A publisher (a key, a socket and `:publish?`) deposits its HEAD's\n     trace on its board (`yin.vm.linker.head/deposit!`) at every HEAD\n     write, after `announce!`, at startup for a recovered HEAD, and once\n     when a hydration completes (5.4).  Once its socket is bound on a\n     loopback literal it serves the board at the same port number\n     (`yin.vm.linker.head.board/serve`) and prints the join\n     token `yin:<host:port>/<principal>` when that endpoint is bound.\n   * A reader (`:follow`, each `{:principal hex :host h :port p}` with a\n     loopback host) restores `<dir>/heads.edn` before any connection\n     (5.7), dials each principal's board at the address it was given and\n     nowhere else, steps the follower after the node, and installs a\n     confirmed head only after `heads.edn` holds it.\n\n   Shutdown closes each of those resources once, under this namespace's\n   ownership: `stop!` asks, `step` completes, `stopped?` reports, and\n   `close!` is the bounded drain's last resort.  A host never reaches\n   past this seam to a board, a dial or a socket.\"\n  (:require #?(:cljd [clojure.edn :as edn]\n               :clj [clojure.edn :as edn]\n               :cljs [cljs.reader :as reader])\n            [clojure.string :as str]\n            [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [dao.space.dht :as dht]\n            [dao.space.index :as space.index]\n            [dao.space.store :as durable]\n            [dao.space.store.fs :as fs]\n            [dao.stream :as stream]\n            [dao.stream.datagram :as datagram]\n            [dao.stream.remote-channel :as remote-channel]\n            [dao.stream.ringbuffer :as ringbuffer]\n            [yin.repl :as repl]\n            [yin.repl.connect :as connect]\n            [yin.shell.registry :as shell.registry]\n            [yin.vm.linker.dht :as ld]\n            [yin.vm.linker.head :as head]\n            [yin.vm.linker.head.board :as head.board]\n            [yin.vm.linker.interpreter :as link]\n            [yin.vm.linker.pair :as link.pair]\n            [yin.vm.linker.sign :as sign]))\n\n\n(def default-max-inbound-bytes\n  \"The inbound storage bound `dao.space.dht` itself defaults to.\"\n  dht/default-max-inbound-bytes)\n\n\n(def cli-max-inbound-bytes\n  \"The CLI default of the inbound storage bound: 64 MiB of payload a\n   publishing node accepts from peers' `:store` requests before it\n   refuses them explicitly.  `--dht-max-inbound-bytes` overrides it.\"\n  (* 64 1024 1024))\n\n\n(def default-repair-slots\n  \"The repair writes this store's node holds outstanding at once: three\n   times `dao.space.dht`'s default and still below its pending-write\n   bound, so the node draws no `/busy` and fresh writes keep a bound of\n   their own while a repair is queued.  This store publishes 64-key index\n   pages (`compose`), so a round carries about twice the addresses the\n   generic 512-key page did, and every live publication repairs its own:\n   a write no peer accepts holds its slot for the DHT's whole `ack-ticks`,\n   so at 16 slots one cycle of a full `:max-repairing` set outlasts the\n   cycle's own period, the node's queues never empty, and the host ticker\n   never leaves its base cadence (`busy?`, yin.repl.main/moved?).  One\n   cycle drains inside the shortest period again at three times the\n   slots.\n\n   Repair capacity stays measurement-driven\n   (docs/design/yin.repl.dht-composition.md sections 6 and 12, R4/R6):\n   no entitlement attaches to this number, and step 5 of that sequence\n   re-measures it in this composition.\"\n  48)\n\n\n(def heads-file\n  \"The follower's durable state in the store directory (5.7).\"\n  \"heads.edn\")\n\n\n(def heads-max-bytes\n  \"The most bytes a `heads.edn` may hold: 64 followed principals take a\n   few tens of KiB, so a larger file is refused before it is decoded.\"\n  (* 1024 1024))\n\n\n(defn- refused-head\n  \"Why a reader cannot hydrate `manifest` into a directory whose own HEAD\n   already names `recovered`, or nil.  Hydration never replaces an index\n   the directory holds.\"\n  [dir manifest recovered]\n  (when (and manifest recovered (not= manifest recovered))\n    (str dir \" already holds an index (HEAD names \" recovered \"); hydrate \"\n         manifest \" into an empty directory\")))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The store spec (docs/design/yin.repl.dht-composition.md section 6: the\n;; scheme parsing and the checked spec move out of the engine's store\n;; selection into the composition's own factory)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n\n    (not (and (integer? bind-port) (<= 0 bind-port 65535)))\n    \"the DHT bind port must be 0 (ephemeral) or 1 to 65535\"\n\n    (not (and (integer? max-inbound-bytes) (<= 0 max-inbound-bytes)))\n    \"--dht-max-inbound-bytes must be a nonnegative integer\"\n\n    (and (empty? peers) (some? manifest))\n    (str \"--dht-manifest needs a --dht-peer to fetch from: with no peers \"\n         \"the DHT store is solo and opens no socket\")\n\n    (not (or (nil? manifest) (jing/segment-address? manifest)))\n    \"--dht-manifest must be a segment manifest address\"\n\n    (not (or (nil? bind!) (fn? bind!)))\n    \":bind! must be a dao.stream.datagram host seam function\"))\n\n\n(def dht-defaults\n  \"The DHT store spec's defaults: solo (no peers), publishing off, bound\n   to loopback on an ephemeral port, the default inbound bound, and no\n   manifest to hydrate.\"\n  {:peers []\n   :publish? false\n   :bind-host \"127.0.0.1\"\n   :bind-port 0\n   :max-inbound-bytes cli-max-inbound-bytes\n   :manifest nil})\n\n\n(defn checked-spec\n  \"Validate a `{:type :dht :dir dir}` spec as this composition takes it,\n   `dht-defaults` filled in.  Anything else — a spec of another store's\n   shape, or one this build cannot compose — is refused with the reason.\n\n   The engine's own store selection (`yin.repl.store/checked-spec`) knows\n   `:mem` and `{:type :file :dir dir}` and nothing else: a transport\n   composition checks its own spec here, so the two never disagree about\n   what a `dht:` store is\n   (docs/design/yin.repl.dht-composition.md section 6).\"\n  [spec]\n  ;; jolt: a :file store opens since J4; this composition needs J7's UDP\n  ;; transport, joined in J7\n  (if (and (map? spec) (= :dht (:type spec)))\n    (let [spec (merge dht-defaults spec)]\n      (if-some [refusal (dht-refusal spec)]\n        (throw (ex-info refusal {:spec (dissoc spec :bind!)}))\n        spec))\n    (throw (ex-info (str \"not a dht store spec: \" (pr-str spec)\n                         \"; supported: {:type :dht :dir <dir> ...}\")\n                    {:spec spec\n                                :write! (or write-heads! fs/atomic-replace!)})\n               (and manifest (not= manifest (get-in base [:recovery\n                                                          :manifest])))\n               (-> (dht/load-index manifest)\n                   (assoc ::hydrating manifest)))]\n    ;; a recovered HEAD's trace, so a reader's first contact needs no new\n    ;; round (5.4)\n    (when-some [recovered (and pub (get-in base [:recovery :manifest]))]\n      (deposit-head! pub recovered))\n    [(assoc (dht/store node)\n            ;; The generic publication policy this composition declares\n            ;; (docs/design/yin.repl.dht-composition.md section 5), read\n            ;; off the injected handle by the engine so nothing\n            ;; downstream hardcodes a transport's page size.  Both halves\n            ;; are the transport's own answer (`publication-policy`): the\n            ;; count cap is storage's default, never this composition's\n            ;; message-size override, and the byte ceiling is derived from\n            ;; the bound the node actually composes under.  The store's own\n            ;; full-message refusal stays the last resort, exactly as §5\n            ;; preserves it.\n            :publication-policy (publication-policy node)\n            :head-fn (fn [manifest-address]\n                       ((:head-fn base) manifest-address)\n                       (dht/announce! node manifest-address)\n                       (when pub (deposit-head! pub manifest-address)))\n            :recovery (:recovery base)\n            ;; the reset-retention policy stated explicitly, so the engine\n            ;; never derives it from this composition's directory\n            ;; (docs/design/yin.repl.dht-composition.md section 6)\n            :retain-on-reset? true)\n     node]))\n\n\n(defn- quietly!\n  \"Run the cleanup `f`, swallowing what it throws: a cleanup never\n   replaces the refusal that caused it.\"\n  [f]\n  (try (f) nil\n       (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _ nil)))\n\n\n(defn open\n  \"Open a `dht:<dir>` store from its spec (`checked-spec`): the durable\n   directory store, and the `dao.space.dht` node joined over it.\n\n   Answers the two values of section 3's ownership split, and never one\n   value carrying both:\n\n   ```clojure\n   {:index-store <the generic dao.jing handle the engine is constructed\n                  with, carrying :head-fn, :recovery, :publication-policy\n                  and :retain-on-reset?>\n    :node <this composition's transport step-owner value>\n    :recovery <what the open validated, {:manifest :datoms}>\n    :publication-policy <the immutable generic policy of section 5>\n    :retain-index-on-reset? true}\n   ```\n\n   `opts`: `:key`, the publisher key `{:seed :public}`, which makes a\n   publishing node deposit its head; `:ws`, the host WebSocket seam\n   `{:connect! :bind! :unbind!}` (yin.repl.host) the board is served and\n   dialed over; `:write-heads!`, `(fn [dir name text])`, the atomic file\n   replace `heads.edn` is written with (`dao.space.store.fs` by\n   default); `:open-store`, `(fn [dir])`, the durable directory open\n   (`dao.space.store/open` by default).\n\n   A refusal releases everything it opened, exactly once, and is\n   answered as itself whatever the release throws: before the join the\n   directory store is closed; once joined the node owns it, and closing\n   the node closes the socket and then the store.\"\n  ([spec] (open spec {}))\n  ([spec {:keys [key ws write-heads! open-store]}]\n   (let [{:keys [dir peers publish? bind-host bind-port max-inbound-bytes\n                 manifest bind!]\n          :as checked} (checked-spec spec)\n         follow (checked-follow checked)\n         base ((or open-store durable/open) dir)\n         [node records]\n         (try\n           (when-some [why (refused-head dir manifest\n                                         (get-in base [:recovery :manifest]))]\n             (throw (ex-info why {:dir dir :manifest manifest})))\n           (let [records (heads-records dir follow)]\n             [(dht/join {:local base\n                         :peers peers\n                         :publish? publish?\n                         :bind-host bind-host\n                         :bind-port bind-port\n                         :max-inbound-bytes max-inbound-bytes\n                         :repair-slots default-repair-slots\n                         :bind! (or bind! (dht/default-bind))})\n              records])\n           (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\n             (quietly! #(durable/close! base))\n             (throw e)))]\n     ;; from here the node owns the store and holds a socket\n     (try\n       (let [[index-store node]\n             (compose node base checked follow records\n                      {:key key :ws ws :write-heads! write-heads!})]\n         {:index-store index-store\n          :node node\n          :recovery (:recovery index-store)\n          :publication-policy (:publication-policy index-store)\n          :retain-index-on-reset? true})\n       (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\n         (quietly! #(dht/close! node))\n         (throw e))))))\n\n\n(defn follower\n  \"The head follower a composition's `node` carries, or nil when it\n   follows nothing.\"\n  [node]\n  (get node ::follower))\n\n\n(defn stop!\n  \"Initiate the head composition's exit on `session`'s node: the board's\n   server is asked to stop (`head.board/stop!`), every follow dial is\n   closed and no dial is composed again, and the node is marked\n   stopping.  Performs no other I/O; `step` completes the stop and\n   `stopped?` reports it.  A session without a node, or one already\n   stopping, is answered unchanged.  The board serves a read-only ring\n   that stop does not end, so a reader has nothing to be told but the\n   reattachable close its connection observes.\n\n   This composition is the one resource owner: a host asks once, here,\n   and never closes a board, a dial or the store itself\n   (docs/design/yin.repl.dht-composition.md sections 3 and 6).\"\n  [session]\n  (let [node (:node session)]\n    (if (or (nil? node) (::stopping? node))\n      session\n      (do (doseq [[_ {:keys [dial]}] (get-in node [::follow :links])]\n            (when dial (head.board/close! dial)))\n          (assoc session :node\n                 (cond-> (assoc node ::stopping? true)\n                   (get-in node [::publisher :server])\n                   (update-in [::publisher :server] head.board/stop!)\n\n                   (::follow node)\n                   (assoc-in [::follow :links] {})))))))\n\n\n(defn stopped?\n  \"True when the head composition owes nothing at exit: no node, no\n   board server, or a board server that is stopped or refused.\"\n  [session]\n  (let [server (get-in session [:node ::publisher :server])]\n    (or (nil? server) (head.board/stopped? server))))\n\n\n(defn close!\n  \"The last resort after the exit drain's budget\n   (`yin.repl.main/stop-ticks`): whatever the board still owes is asked\n   for once more and not awaited.  The ordinary exit is `stop!`, then\n   `step` until `stopped?`.  A session never stopped is stopped first, so\n   a direct caller (a test, an embedder without a drain) still closes the\n   dials; the board's stopping tick runs at the node's last tick reading\n   only when the board is not already `stopped?`.\n\n   The store's own resources are the durable handle's `:close-fn`, which\n   the composition's single resource owner releases after this\n   (`yin.shell.main/close-composition!`), so nothing here is closed\n   twice.\"\n  [session]\n  (let [session (stop! session)\n        node (:node session)\n        server (get-in node [::publisher :server])]\n    (when (and server (not (head.board/stopped? server)))\n      (head.board/serve-step server (or (::now node) 0)))\n    nil))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Lines\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- address-text\n  [host port]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1940,2040p;2095,2150p' Makefile; cat host/gambit/yin-repl-driver.ss; cat host/gambit/ffi/watchdog.sh\"","aggregated_output":"\t    echo \"gambitseedcheck: host/gambit/seed is STALE — run 'make gambitseed'\" >&2; \\\n\t    rm -rf \"$$out\"; exit 1; \\\n\t  fi\n\n# Skip before building the native bridge when Gambit is absent. Explicit\n# gambitffi* targets still require an installation and local provenance.\ngambitcheck: $(if $(wildcard $(GAMBIT_GSI)),gambitffi-bridge)\n\t@if [ -x \"$(GAMBIT_GSI)\" ]; then \\\n\t\tsh host/gambit/ffi/watchdog.sh 120 \"$(GAMBIT_GSI)\" host/gambit/gambitcheck.ss && \\\n\t\tsh host/gambit/ffi/watchdog.sh 180 \"$(GAMBIT_GSI)\" host/gambit/ffi/gambitffi-public.ss; \\\n\telse \\\n\t\t$(GAMBIT-SKIP); \\\n\tfi\n\n# G2 kernel-test gate (jolt-mj95.4): the full booted manifest on native gsi,\n# driven through the real natives. Same detection-gated shape as gambitcheck;\n# a second on gsi. Run from the repo root (boot's irregex load is cwd-relative).\ngambitkernel:\n\t@if [ -x \"$(GAMBIT_GSI)\" ]; then \\\n\t\tsh host/gambit/ffi/watchdog.sh 120 \"$(GAMBIT_GSI)\" host/gambit/kernel-test.ss; \\\n\telse \\\n\t\t$(GAMBIT-SKIP); \\\n\tfi\n\n# G3 eval gate: real jolt source through jolt-compile-eval on the booted\n# manifest + cross-minted seed, renders pinned to Chez captures. Detection-\n# gated like gambitcheck. In the ci list: it is the only gate that runs\n# emitted code on gsi end to end, and while it sat outside ci every row failed\n# for two weeks (jolt-cw2p) with nothing red. Run from the repo root.\ngambiteval:\n\t@if [ -x \"$(GAMBIT_GSI)\" ]; then \\\n\t\tsh host/gambit/ffi/watchdog.sh 180 \"$(GAMBIT_GSI)\" host/gambit/eval-test.ss; \\\n\telse \\\n\t\t$(GAMBIT-SKIP); \\\n\tfi\n\n# The source-loader witness is self-contained. The full Yin gate reads a separate\n# datomworld checkout and stays opt-in while its native host closure is incomplete.\ngambitsourceload:\n\t@if [ -x \"$(GAMBIT_GSI)\" ]; then \\\n\t\tsh host/gambit/ffi/watchdog.sh 240 \"$(GAMBIT_GSI)\" host/gambit/source-loader-test.ss; \\\n\telse \\\n\t\t$(GAMBIT-SKIP); \\\n\tfi\n\nDATOMWORLD_ROOT ?= ../datomworld\ngambitdaobytes:\n\tsh host/gambit/ffi/watchdog.sh 240 \"$(GAMBIT_GSI)\" host/gambit/source-loader-test.ss \"$(abspath $(DATOMWORLD_ROOT))\"\n\n# The interpreted bootstrap is available through yin-repl-main.ss. Compiling\n# the same boot makes the full app closure fit the acceptance watchdog.\nGAMBIT_YIN_BOOT ?=\ngambityinrepl: gambitffi-bridge\n\t@yin_root=\"$(abspath $(DATOMWORLD_ROOT))\"; \\\n\tif [ ! -f \"$$yin_root/src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc\" ]; then \\\n\t  echo \"gambityinrepl: no Yin source entry under $$yin_root. Set DATOMWORLD_ROOT to a Datomworld checkout.\" >&2; \\\n\t  exit 2; \\\n\tfi; \\\n\tboot=\"$(GAMBIT_YIN_BOOT)\"; \\\n\tif [ -z \"$$boot\" ]; then \\\n\t  boot=$$(sh host/gambit/ffi/watchdog.sh 2400 python3 host/gambit/build-yin-boot.py \\\n\t    \"$(GAMBIT_GSC)\" \"$(abspath build/gambit-yin)\") || exit $$?; \\\n\tfi; \\\n\tJOLT_GAMBIT_YIN_BOOT=\"$$boot\" sh test/gambit/yin-repl-session.sh \\\n\t  \"$(GAMBIT_GSI)\" \"$$yin_root\" host/gambit/yin-repl-native.ss\n\n# Every Scheme global the full-profile boot references is defined — Gambit's\n# own linker report over the compiled boot, minus what the record translator\n# evals at runtime, against host/gambit/unbound-allowlist.txt (untaken paths;\n# a stale line fails). The boot splices most of host/chez, so a Chez-side name\n# reaching a shared file is otherwise an unbound global no Chez gate can see;\n# mirrordrift only compares names defined on BOTH hosts. It compiles the seed\n# to JS text; time varies by compiler build. Detection-gated. Ordered\n# after gambitboot: both regenerate boot-full.ss, and under -j a reader must\n# not open the file mid-rewrite.\ngambitunbound: gambitboot\n\t@if [ -x \"$(GAMBIT_GSC)\" ]; then \\\n\t\tJOLT_GSC=\"$(GAMBIT_GSC)\" sh host/gambit/unbound-check.sh; \\\n\telse \\\n\t\t$(GAMBIT-SKIP); \\\n\tfi\n\ngambitunbound-regen:\n\t@JOLT_GSC=\"$(GAMBIT_GSC)\" sh host/gambit/unbound-check.sh --regen\n\n# The jolt half of the same question: every var cell the boot interned is\n# bound. Emitted code reaches a var through its cell, so after the boot the\n# var table holds every var the seed and the compiler image reference — an\n# unbound root is a reference nothing this boot defines (clojure.core/\n# chunk-first, bound by the excluded natives-array.ss, is how every `defn`\n# died). Against host/gambit/unbound-vars-allowlist.txt; a stale line fails.\n# One gsi boot, a few seconds. Detection-gated.\ngambitvars:\n\t@if [ -x \"$(GAMBIT_GSI)\" ]; then \\\n\t\t\"$(GAMBIT_GSI)\" host/gambit/unbound-vars.ss < /dev/null; \\\n\telse \\\n\t\t$(GAMBIT-SKIP); \\\n\tfi\n\ngambitvars-regen:\n\t@JOLT_GAMBITVARS=regen \"$(GAMBIT_GSI)\" host/gambit/unbound-vars.ss < /dev/null\n\tfi\n\n# ---- P1b-stage1: the gambitffi* gates ----------------------------------------\n#\n# The bounded FFI tier qualified per the standing codex C4 PARTIAL-GO ruling:\n# memory ops, loading, ordinary calls and invocation-local errno capture,\n# behind the ahead-of-time native bridge (host/gambit/ffi/joltffi-bridge.scm,\n# compiled by gsc into build/gambit-ffi/joltffi-bridge.o1). Blocking,\n# callables, runtime signatures and object pinning keep their message-carrying\n# refusals; the gates assert both the witnesses AND the retained refusals.\n#\n# Deterministic bootstrap: every artifact builds from the pinned inputs in\n# host/gambit/ffi/DIGESTS.txt — verified before compiling, output digests\n# recorded after. No ambient .o1/.dylib is ever trusted. The aggregate target\n# is fail-closed: it passes only when all three gate scripts pass, and a gate\n# runs under the P1a-r2 external watchdog (host/gambit/ffi/watchdog.sh, the\n# same pattern the p1a witnesses use) so a frozen VM is a recorded failure,\n# never a hang.\n\nGAMBIT-FFI-DIR := host/gambit/ffi\nGAMBIT-FFI-BUILD := build/gambit-ffi\n\n# Keep Darwin's expanded link commands unchanged. Reject other hosts when an\n# FFI fixture recipe is expanded, without disabling unrelated make targets.\nGAMBIT-FFI-HOST-OS := $(shell uname -s)\nifeq ($(GAMBIT-FFI-HOST-OS),Darwin)\nGAMBIT-FFI-SHARED-FLAGS := -dynamiclib\nGAMBIT-FFI-ERRNO-ACCESSOR := __error\nelse ifeq ($(GAMBIT-FFI-HOST-OS),Linux)\nGAMBIT-FFI-SHARED-FLAGS := -shared -fPIC\nGAMBIT-FFI-ERRNO-ACCESSOR := __errno_location\nelse\nGAMBIT-FFI-SHARED-FLAGS = $(error gambitffi: unsupported fixture-link platform '$(GAMBIT-FFI-HOST-OS)' (expected Darwin or Linux))\nGAMBIT-FFI-ERRNO-ACCESSOR = $(error gambitffi: unsupported errno-accessor platform '$(GAMBIT-FFI-HOST-OS)' (expected Darwin or Linux))\nendif\n\n# the pinned inputs must match the recorded digests BEFORE anything compiles\ngambitffi-verify-digests:\n\t@sh $(GAMBIT-FFI-DIR)/verify-digests.sh \"$(GAMBIT_PREFIX)\"\n\n$(GAMBIT-FFI-BUILD)/joltffi-bridge.o1: gambitffi-force $(GAMBIT-FFI-DIR)/joltffi-bridge.scm \\\n\t\t$(GAMBIT-FFI-DIR)/DIGESTS.txt $(GAMBIT-FFI-DIR)/generated-shims.scm\n\t@$(MAKE) --no-print-directory gambitffi-verify-digests\n\t@mkdir -p $(GAMBIT-FFI-BUILD)\n\t@rm -f $(GAMBIT-FFI-BUILD)/joltffi-bridge.o1 $(GAMBIT-FFI-BUILD)/joltffi-bridge.o[0-9]* \\\n\t       $(GAMBIT-FFI-BUILD)/joltffi-bridge.c $(GAMBIT-FFI-BUILD)/joltffi-bridge.o \\\n\t       $(GAMBIT-FFI-BUILD)/joltffi-bridge.scm\n\tcp $(GAMBIT-FFI-DIR)/joltffi-bridge.scm $(GAMBIT-FFI-DIR)/generated-shims.scm $(GAMBIT-FFI-BUILD)/\n\tcd $(GAMBIT-FFI-BUILD) && sh ../../$(GAMBIT-FFI-DIR)/watchdog.sh 120 $(GAMBIT_GSC) -dynamic -keep-temp joltffi-bridge.scm\n\t@shasum -a 256 $(GAMBIT-FFI-BUILD)/joltffi-bridge.o1 \\\n\t  > $(GAMBIT-FFI-BUILD)/joltffi-bridge.o1.sha256\n\t@cc --version > $(GAMBIT-FFI-BUILD)/compiler.txt\n\t@shasum -a 256 $(GAMBIT_GSC) $(GAMBIT_GSI) \"$(GAMBIT_PREFIX)/include/gambit.h\" \\\n\t  \"$(GAMBIT_PREFIX)/lib/libgambit.a\" > $(GAMBIT-FFI-BUILD)/runtime.sha256\n\n$(GAMBIT-FFI-BUILD)/libjoltffi-fixture.dylib: gambitffi-force $(GAMBIT-FFI-DIR)/joltffi-fixture.c \\\n;; The native and interpreted boots use the same source loader and Yin owner.\n(##include \"host-apply.ss\")\n(##include \"source-loader.ss\")\n(##include \"unbound-vars-lib.ss\")\n(##include \"yin/blake3.ss\")\n\n(define (gambit-yin-random-bytes size)\n  (let* ((n (jnum->exact size))\n         (out (make-u8vector n)))\n    (call-with-input-file \"/dev/urandom\"\n      (lambda (port)\n        (unless (= (read-subu8vector out 0 n port) n)\n          (error \"Gambit could not read OS entropy\"))))\n    out))\n\n;; A single outstanding request keeps the input reader from filling the\n;; mailbox. Only Yin's owner interprets submitted lines.\n(define (gambit-yin-wake)\n  (vector\n    (thread-start!\n      (make-thread\n        (lambda ()\n          (let loop ()\n            (let* ((owner (thread-receive))\n                   (line (read-line)))\n              (thread-send owner (if (eof-object? line) jolt-nil line))\n              (unless (eof-object? line) (loop)))))))\n    #f))\n\n(define (gambit-yin-close-wake! wake)\n  (thread-terminate! (vector-ref wake 0))\n  jolt-nil)\n\n(define (gambit-yin-wait-input! wake ms on-line)\n  (let ((seconds (/ ms 1000.0)))\n    (if (jolt-nil? on-line)\n        (thread-sleep! seconds)\n        (begin\n          (unless (vector-ref wake 1)\n            (thread-send (vector-ref wake 0) (current-thread))\n            (vector-set! wake 1 #t))\n          (let ((line (thread-receive seconds #f)))\n            (when line\n              (vector-set! wake 1 #f)\n              (jolt-invoke on-line line))))))\n  jolt-nil)\n\n(set! jolt-random-bytes gambit-yin-random-bytes)\n(register-class-statics! \"System\"\n  (list (cons \"currentTimeMillis\" gambit-epoch-ms)\n        (cons \"exit\" (lambda (status) (exit (jnum->exact status))))))\n\n(let ((args (cdr (command-line))))\n  (unless (pair? args)\n    (display \"usage: yin-repl-main.ss DATOMWORLD_ROOT [Yin arguments...]\\n\" (current-error-port))\n    (exit 2))\n  (let ((root (path-expand (car args)))\n        (jolt-root (current-directory)))\n    (unless (file-exists? (path-expand \"src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc\" root))\n      (fprintf (current-error-port) \"yin.repl: no source entry under ~a\\n\" root)\n      (exit 2))\n    (with-exception-catcher\n      (lambda (e)\n        (let* ((value (jolt-unwrap-throw e))\n               (message (jolt-throwable-message value))\n               (location (jolt-current-source-string)))\n          (fprintf (current-error-port) \"yin.repl startup failed: ~a\\n\"\n                   (if (string? message) message (jolt-pr-readable value)))\n          (when (and (string? location) (not (string=? location \"\")))\n            (fprintf (current-error-port) \"Yin source: ~a\\n\" location))\n          (exit 1)))\n      (lambda ()\n        ;; Cache the qualified bridge before resolving Yin's paths relative to\n        ;; its own checkout. The source loader itself uses absolute roots.\n        (joltffi-ensure-bridge! 'yin.repl)\n        (let ((load-ns\n                (gambit-install-source-loader!\n                  (list (list (path-expand \"host/gambit/yin\" jolt-root) \".jolt\")\n                        (list (path-expand \"src/jolt\" root) \".jolt\" \".cljc\" \".clj\")\n                        (list (path-expand \"src/cljc\" root) \".cljc\" \".clj\")\n                        (list (path-expand \"src/clj\" root) \".clj\")\n                        (list (path-expand \"stdlib\" jolt-root) \".jolt\" \".cljc\" \".clj\")\n                        (list (path-expand \"jolt-core\" jolt-root) \".cljc\" \".clj\"))\n                  '(\"clojure.core\" \"jolt.ffi\"))))\n          (parameterize ((current-directory root))\n            (let* ((audit? (getenv \"JOLT_YIN_AUDIT\" #f))\n                   (before (and audit? (unbound-var-names))))\n              (dynamic-wind\n                (lambda () #f)\n                (lambda () (load-ns \"jolt.gambit.yin\" #f))\n                (lambda ()\n                  (when audit?\n                    (let ((added (filter (lambda (name) (not (member name before)))\n                                         (unbound-var-names))))\n                      (fprintf (current-error-port) \"yin source var audit: ~a newly unbound\\n\"\n                               (length added))\n                      (for-each (lambda (name) (fprintf (current-error-port) \"  ~a\\n\" name))\n                                added))))))\n            (def-var! \"yin.repl\" \"host-text\" (string-append \"Host: jolt / Gambit \" (system-version-string)))\n            (let ((main (var-deref \"jolt.gambit.yin\" \"-main\")))\n              (unless (procedure? main)\n                (error \"jolt.gambit.yin/-main did not load\"))\n              (apply jolt-invoke main (cdr args)))))))))\n#!/bin/sh\n# External deadline kills the gate process group. Keep stdin for the command.\nexec python3 -c '\nimport os, signal, subprocess, sys\nseconds = float(sys.argv[1])\np = subprocess.Popen(sys.argv[2:], start_new_session=True)\ntry:\n    status = p.wait(timeout=seconds)\nexcept subprocess.TimeoutExpired:\n    os.killpg(p.pid, signal.SIGKILL)\n    p.wait()\n    print(\"WATCHDOG: TIMEOUT after %ss: %s\" % (seconds, \" \".join(sys.argv[2:])), file=sys.stderr)\n    sys.exit(124)\nsys.exit(status if status >= 0 else 128 - status)\n' \"$@\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,275p' host/gambit/host-statics.ss; sed -n '320,480p' host/gambit/host-ffi.ss; cat host/gambit/ffi/joltffi-bridge.scm\"","aggregated_output":";; host-statics.ss — the Gambit target's host-interop tier: the jhost record,\n;; the method registry and the (.method obj) arm over it, the entry points the\n;; emitter lowers Class/member and (new Class) to, and the statics and\n;; constructors the SEED reaches.\n;;\n;; On Chez this is host-static.ss (registries, jhost, entry points),\n;; host-static-methods.ss (every Class/member) and host-static-classes.ss\n;; (every instantiable class) — the java.lang/util/io surface portable cljc\n;; code calls. This target boots none of those; what it carries is the\n;; registries in the same shape, so the shared java/ files that register into\n;; them (class-model.ss, string-builder.ss) load unchanged, plus the members\n;; clojure.core and the embedded stdlib call — the analyzer emits them as\n;; Scheme-level (host-static-call \"Class\" \"member\" …) forms, so each is either\n;; registered here or a raise naming the class. `make gambitstatics` pins the\n;; set: every static and constructor the seed emits resolves, or is a\n;; classified line in seed-statics-allowlist.txt.\n;;\n;; Loaded before the prelude, after records-gambit.ss (the dispatch-arm\n;; registry) and java-parse.ss (the NumberFormatException family); the shared\n;; class-model.ss and string-builder.ss follow it.\n\n;; ---- host object --------------------------------------------------------------\n;; The generic carrier for a host value: a tag naming its method table, and\n;; mutable state. A Class object is a jhost tagged \"class\" (class-model.ss), a\n;; StringBuilder one tagged \"string-builder\" (string-builder.ss).\n(define-record-type jhost (fields tag (mutable state)) (nongenerative gambit-jhost-v1))\n(define-record-type (jolt-obj-array make-jolt-obj-array jolt-obj-array?) (fields vec))\n\n;; A question a host object's tag alone answers, remembered by the tag object —\n;; the same helper as host/chez/java/host-static.ss (which explains it); the\n;; shared java/ files (tree-map.ss, byte-buffer.ss, …) define predicates with it.\n(define (jhost-tag-memo test)\n  (let ((hit '(#f . #f)) (miss1 '(#f . #f)) (miss2 '(#f . #f)))\n    (lambda (x)\n      (and (jhost? x)\n           (let ((t (jhost-tag x)))\n             (let ((h hit))\n               (cond ((eq? (car h) t) (cdr h))\n                     ((eq? (car miss1) t) #f)\n                     ((eq? (car miss2) t) #f)\n                     (else\n                      (let ((a (test t)))\n                        (if a\n                            (set! hit (cons t a))\n                            (begin (set! miss2 miss1) (set! miss1 (cons t #f))))\n                        a)))))))))\n(define-syntax define-jhost-tag-pred\n  (syntax-rules ()\n    ((_ (name t) body ...) (define name (jhost-tag-memo (lambda (t) body ...))))))\n\n;; ---- registries ---------------------------------------------------------------\n;; class-ctors-tbl / register-class-ctor! are rt-core.ss's (a deftype registers\n;; its ctor there under its tag); class-statics-tbl / register-class-statics!\n;; are prelude-shims.ss's (compile-eval.ss registers the Compiler statics into\n;; it at load). The method registry is this file's.\n(define host-methods-tbl (make-hashtable string-hash string=?))   ; tag -> (method-ht)\n(define (register-host-methods! tag members)\n  (let ((h (or (hashtable-ref host-methods-tbl tag #f)\n               (let ((h (make-hashtable string-hash string=?)))\n                 (hashtable-set! host-methods-tbl tag h) h))))\n    (for-each (lambda (p) (hashtable-set! h (car p) (cdr p))) members)))\n;; The member NAME resolves to on TAG, or #f. There is no parent chain here:\n;; every jhost tag this target builds registers its own members.\n(define (host-method-ref tag name)\n  (let ((h (hashtable-ref host-methods-tbl tag #f)))\n    (and h (hashtable-ref h name #f))))\n;; A class token may arrive fully qualified (java.io.StringReader) or short\n;; (StringReader): exact first, then by last dotted segment.\n(define (lookup-class h-tbl name)\n  (or (hashtable-ref h-tbl name #f)\n      (hashtable-ref h-tbl (short-class-name name) #f)))\n\n;; (.method obj …) on a jhost: the tag's table, else — for a Class token — the\n;; statics of the class it names, which is what the form means on the JVM for\n;; an imported simple name; a leading dash is the explicit FIELD spelling.\n(register-method-arm! arm-priority-host-type\n  (lambda (obj method-name rest-args)\n    (cond\n      ((jhost? obj)\n       (let* ((mh (hashtable-ref host-methods-tbl (jhost-tag obj) #f))\n              (f (and mh (hashtable-ref mh method-name #f)))\n              (args (if (jolt-nil? rest-args) '() (seq->list rest-args))))\n         (cond\n           (f (apply f obj args))\n           ((string=? (jhost-tag obj) \"class\")\n            (let* ((mname (if (and (> (string-length method-name) 1)\n                                   (char=? (string-ref method-name 0) #\\-))\n                              (substring method-name 1 (string-length method-name))\n                              method-name))\n                   (v (host-static-ref (jclass-name obj) mname)))\n              (cond ((procedure? v) (apply v args))\n                    ((null? args) v)\n                    (else (throw-jvm (quote IllegalArgumentException)\n                            (string-append (jclass-name obj) \"/\" mname\n                                           \" is a static field; it takes no arguments\"))))))\n           (else (dispatch-miss obj method-name args)))))\n      (else 'pass))))\n\n;; ---- emit entry points --------------------------------------------------------\n;; (Class/member args) is emitted as (host-static-call \"Class\" \"member\" args), a\n;; static field read as (host-static-ref …), (new Class args) as (host-new\n;; \"Class\" args) — Scheme-level calls in the seed, not var references, so the\n;; three are GLOBALS here as well as clojure.core vars. A miss raises a named\n;; UnsupportedOperationException: the member is one this target does not shim,\n;; and the call site says which.\n(define host-static-miss (list 'host-static-miss))\n(define (host-static-lookup class member)\n  (let ((h (lookup-class class-statics-tbl class)))\n    (if h (hashtable-ref h member host-static-miss) host-static-miss)))\n;; (.getName String) — an instance member on a class token — is a call on the\n;; java.lang.Class OBJECT when the class has no such static, as on the JVM.\n(define (class-instance-fallback class member)\n  (let ((h (hashtable-ref host-methods-tbl \"class\" #f)))\n    (and h\n         (let ((m (hashtable-ref h member #f)))\n           (and m (lambda args (apply m (jolt-class-for class) args)))))))\n(define (host-static-ref class member)\n  (let ((v (host-static-lookup class member)))\n    (if (eq? v host-static-miss)\n        (or (and (jch-known? class) (class-instance-fallback class member))\n            (jolt-throw\n              (jolt-host-throwable\n                \"java.lang.UnsupportedOperationException\"\n                (string-append class \"/\" member\n                               \" is unsupported on the gambit target: no shim registers it\"\n                               \" (host/gambit/host-statics.ss)\"))))\n        v)))\n(define (host-static-call class member . args)\n  ;; a procedure is a method to call, anything else a field value — which\n  ;; answers a zero-argument access and nothing more\n  (let ((v (host-static-ref class member)))\n    (cond ((procedure? v) (apply v args))\n          ((null? args) v)\n          (else (throw-jvm (quote IllegalArgumentException)\n                  (string-append class \"/\" member \" is a static field; it takes no arguments\"))))))\n;; The per-site entry points the shared emitter uses when it has a const pool\n;; (Chez caches in them: host/chez/java/host-static.ss). Here they answer through\n;; the uncached lookups, which is the same answer.\n(define (host-static-site-make) (vector #f))\n(define (host-static-ref-site site class member) (host-static-ref class member))\n(define (host-static-proc-site site class member n)\n  (lambda args (apply host-static-call class member args)))\n(def-var! \"clojure.core\" \"host-static-call\" host-static-call)\n(def-var! \"clojure.core\" \"host-static-ref\" host-static-ref)\n;; (Class/member) with no arguments is a field read when a field is registered\n;; and a no-arg call otherwise — the analyzer's jolt.host/static-member.\n(def-var! \"jolt.host\" \"static-member\"\n  (lambda (class member)\n    (let ((v (host-static-ref class member)))\n      (if (procedure? v) (v) v))))\n\n;; Two kinds of class exist on this target beyond the shims registered below:\n;; the exception hierarchy, which every core throw site constructs ((new\n;; ClassCastException msg) is what (zero? \"a\") raises), and a deftype/defrecord,\n;; whose type name is a var holding its ctor — the same two arms Chez's host-new\n;; (host-static.ss) takes. The throwable ctors are derived from the ONE\n;; hierarchy in class-hierarchy.ss the way host-static-classes.ss derives them,\n;; so (E. msg), (E. msg cause), (E. cause) and (E.) all build the typed\n;; throwable. Anything else names a class this target has no shim for, and says so.\n(define (gambit-exc-ctor canonical)\n  (lambda args\n    (let* ((a0 (if (pair? args) (car args) jolt-nil))\n           (rest (if (pair? args) (cdr args) '()))\n           (cause (if (pair? rest) (car rest) jolt-nil)))\n      (cond\n        ((string? a0) (jolt-host-throwable canonical a0 cause))\n        ((jolt-nil? a0) (jolt-host-throwable canonical jolt-nil))\n        ;; (E. cause): the message is the cause's toString, as on the JVM\n        ((and (null? rest) (ex-info-map? a0))\n         (jolt-host-throwable canonical (jolt-str-render-one a0) a0))\n        (else (jolt-host-throwable canonical (jolt-str-render-one a0) cause))))))\n(let-values (((keys vals) (hashtable-entries jvm-class-parents)))\n  (vector-for-each\n    (lambda (canonical supers)\n      (when (jch-isa? canonical \"Throwable\")\n        (let ((short (jch-last-segment canonical)))\n          (register-class-ctor! short (gambit-exc-ctor canonical))\n          (unless (string=? short canonical)\n            (register-class-ctor! canonical (gambit-exc-ctor canonical))))))\n    keys vals))\n(define (host-new class . args)\n  (let ((ctor (lookup-class class-ctors-tbl class)))\n    (cond\n      (ctor (apply ctor args))\n      (else\n       (let ((cell (or (var-cell-lookup (chez-current-ns) class)\n                       (var-cell-lookup \"clojure.core\" class))))\n         (if (and cell (var-cell-defined? cell) (procedure? (var-cell-root cell)))\n             (apply (var-cell-root cell) args)\n             (jolt-throw\n               (jolt-host-throwable\n                 \"java.lang.UnsupportedOperationException\"\n                 (string-append \"(new \" class \") is unsupported on the gambit target: \"\n                                \"no shim registers the class (host/gambit/host-statics.ss)\")))))))))\n(def-var! \"clojure.core\" \"host-new\" (lambda (c . a) (apply host-new c a)))\n\n;; ---- the statics the seed reaches -----------------------------------------------\n;; Each is the member Chez registers in host-static-methods.ss, with the same\n;; body, so a call answers the same on both targets. Long/parseLong is\n;; cl-format's number parser (java-parse.ss carries the throw);\n;; Character/isWhitespace and String/join are clojure.main's; java.lang.Math is\n;; the shared math.ss, included after this file; the clojure.lang.Util family,\n;; MapEntry/create and Murmur3/hashOrdered are the gvec's (clojure/core/60-gvec.clj);\n;; PersistentList/EMPTY the gvec's empty; RT/REQUIRE_LOCK the object\n;; serialized-require locks. The Compiler statics (munge, demunge, eval) are\n;; registered by compile-eval.ss at its load, into the same table.\n(register-class-statics! \"Long\"\n  (list (cons \"parseLong\" (lambda (s . r) (parse-int-or-throw s (if (null? r) 10 (jnum->exact (car r))) \"long\")))))\n;; JVM Character.isWhitespace: Unicode whitespace MINUS the no-break spaces the\n;; JVM excludes (U+00A0/U+2007/U+202F) and NEL (U+0085), plus U+001C..U+001F.\n(register-class-statics! \"Character\"\n  (list (cons \"isWhitespace\" (lambda (c) (let ((cp (char-code c)))\n                                           (or (and (fx>=? cp #x1C) (fx<=? cp #x1F))\n                                               (and (char-whitespace? (integer->char cp))\n                                                    (not (fx=? cp #xA0)) (not (fx=? cp #x2007)) (not (fx=? cp #x202F))\n                                                    (not (fx=? cp #x85)))))))))\n;; String.join(delim, elems) — elems as a collection or spread as varargs\n(register-class-statics! \"String\"\n  (list (cons \"join\" (lambda (delim . parts)\n                       (let ((items (if (and (fx=? (length parts) 1)\n                                             (not (string? (car parts))))\n                                        (seq->list (jolt-seq (car parts)))\n                                        parts)))\n                         (jolt-str-join-strs (map jolt-str-render-one items)\n                                             (jolt-str-render-one delim)))))))\n(let ((util-statics\n       (list (cons \"hash\" (lambda (x) (if (jolt-nil? x) 0 (record-method-dispatch x \"hashCode\" jolt-nil))))\n             (cons \"hasheq\" (lambda (x) (jolt-hash x)))\n             (cons \"equiv\" (lambda (a b) (if (jolt= a b) #t #f)))\n             (cons \"identical\" (lambda (a b) (if (eq? a b) #t #f)))\n             (cons \"compare\" (lambda (a b) (jolt-compare a b)))\n             (cons \"isInteger\" (lambda (x) (if (and (number? x) (exact? x) (integer? x)) #t #f)))\n             (cons \"equals\" (lambda (a b) (if (jolt= a b) #t #f))))))\n  (register-class-statics! \"Util\" util-statics)\n  (register-class-statics! \"clojure.lang.Util\" util-statics))\n(register-class-statics! \"Murmur3\" (list (cons \"hashOrdered\" (lambda (xs) (hash-ordered (jolt-seq xs))))))\n(register-class-statics! \"MapEntry\" (list (cons \"create\" (lambda (k v) (make-map-entry k v)))))\n(register-class-statics! \"clojure.lang.MapEntry\" (list (cons \"create\" (lambda (k v) (make-map-entry k v)))))\n(register-class-statics! \"PersistentList\" (list (cons \"EMPTY\" jolt-empty-list)))\n(register-class-statics! \"clojure.lang.PersistentList\" (list (cons \"EMPTY\" jolt-empty-list)))\n;; (Object.) — a fresh value with distinct identity: libraries use it as a lock\n;; or a unique sentinel, and RT/REQUIRE_LOCK is one.\n(register-class-ctor! \"Object\" (lambda _ (make-jhost \"object\" (vector))))\n(register-class-arm! (lambda (x) (and (jhost? x) (string=? (jhost-tag x) \"object\")))\n                     (lambda (x) \"java.lang.Object\"))\n(define rt-require-lock (make-jhost \"object\" (vector)))\n(register-class-statics! \"RT\" (list (cons \"REQUIRE_LOCK\" rt-require-lock)))\n(register-class-statics! \"clojure.lang.RT\" (list (cons \"REQUIRE_LOCK\" rt-require-lock)))\n;; System/getProperty: the JVM's spellings, over what the adapter can answer.\n;; os.name follows sa-os-family like Chez's (host-static-methods.ss); a key\n;; nothing here knows answers the default, else nil.\n(define (gambit-get-property k . dflt)\n  (let ((k (jolt-need-string k)))\n    (cond ((string=? k \"os.name\") (case (sa-os-family)\n                                    ((macos) \"Mac OS X\")\n                                    ((windows) \"Windows\")\n                                    (else \"Linux\")))\n          ((string=? k \"line.separator\") \"\\n\")\n          ((string=? k \"file.separator\") \"/\")\n          ((string=? k \"path.separator\") \":\")\n          ((string=? k \"user.dir\") (current-directory))\n          ((string=? k \"user.home\") (or (getenv \"HOME\" #f) \"\"))\n          ((string=? k \"java.io.tmpdir\") (or (getenv \"TMPDIR\" #f) \"/tmp\"))\n          ((pair? dflt) (car dflt))\n          (else jolt-nil))))\n(define (gambit-arraycopy src src-pos dst dst-pos len)\n  (define (check-bounds from-size to-size sp dp n)\n    (unless (and (<= 0 sp (+ sp n) from-size) (<= 0 dp (+ dp n) to-size))\n      (throw-jvm 'ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException \"arraycopy: range outside array\")))\n  (when (or (jolt-nil? src) (jolt-nil? dst))\n    (throw-jvm 'NullPointerException \"arraycopy: null array\"))\n  (let ((sp (jolt-int-cast src-pos))\n        (dp (jolt-int-cast dst-pos))\n        (n (jolt-int-cast len)))\n    (cond\n         (jolt-throw (jolt-ex-info\n                       (string-append \"jolt.ffi/load-library: no :\" key \" entry in the per-OS spec\") spec)))\n       (joltffi-library-map (joltffi-load-candidates (joltffi-candidate-list name)))))\n    (else (joltffi-library-map (joltffi-load-candidates (joltffi-candidate-list (car args)))))))\n\n(define (joltffi-loaded? name)\n  (joltffi-ensure-bridge! 'jolt.ffi/loaded?)\n  (if (joltffi-load-native (joltffi-str-arg \"loaded? name\" name)) #t #f))\n\n(define (joltffi-system-library-candidates n)\n  (let ((n (joltffi-str-arg \"system-library-candidates name\" n)))\n    (list (string-append \"lib\" n \".dylib\") (string-append n \".dylib\"))))\n\n(define (joltffi-load-system-library name)\n  (joltffi-library-map\n   (joltffi-load-candidates\n    (joltffi-system-library-candidates (joltffi-str-arg \"load-system-library name\" name)))))\n\n(define joltffi-find-symbol\n  (case-lambda\n    ((sym)\n     (joltffi-ensure-bridge! 'jolt.ffi/find-symbol)\n     (let* ((n (joltffi-str-arg \"symbol\" sym))\n            (a (joltffi-dlsym-loaded n)))\n       (cond (a a)\n             ((guard (e (#t #f)) (sa-foreign-entry? n)) (sa-foreign-entry-address n))\n             (else jolt-nil))))\n    ((lib sym)\n     (if (jolt-nil? lib)\n         (joltffi-find-symbol sym)\n         (let* ((n (joltffi-str-arg \"symbol\" sym))\n                (path (and (jolt-map? lib)\n                           (jolt-get-dispatch lib (keyword #f \"path\") jolt-nil)))\n                (h (and (string? path) (joltffi-library-handle path))))\n           (unless h\n             (jolt-throw (jolt-ex-info\n                           \"jolt.ffi/find-symbol: no handle for this library — pass the map load-library answered for it\"\n                           (jolt-hash-map (keyword #f \"library\") lib))))\n           (let ((a (joltffi-dlsym h n)))\n             (if (and a (not (= a 0))) a jolt-nil)))))))\n\n;; the dlopen handle a load-library of PATH registered, or #f\n(define (joltffi-library-handle path)\n  (let loop ((hs joltffi-native-handles))\n    (cond ((null? hs) #f)\n          ((string=? (caar hs) path) (cdar hs))\n          (else (loop (cdr hs))))))\n\n(define (joltffi-defining-libraries sym)\n  (joltffi-ensure-bridge! 'jolt.ffi/defining-libraries)\n  (make-pvec\n   (list->vector\n    (map car (joltffi-definers (joltffi-str-arg \"symbol\" sym))))))\n\n(define (joltffi-load-failure-note-var cands)\n  (or (joltffi-load-failure-note\n       (map (lambda (c) (joltffi-str-arg \"load-failure-note candidate\" c))\n            (seq->list (jolt-seq cands))))\n      jolt-nil))\n\n;; ---- refusing rows (message-carrying; the capability stays unqualified) -------\n(define (joltffi-refuse-fn name reason)\n  (lambda args\n    (jolt-throw\n     (jolt-host-throwable\n      \"java.lang.UnsupportedOperationException\"\n      (string-append \"jolt.ffi/\" name \" is unsupported on the gambit target: \" reason)))))\n\n(define (joltffi-refuse-var! name reason)\n  (def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" name (joltffi-refuse-fn name reason)))\n\n;; byte-array moves need the jolt-array runtime (natives-array.ss is excluded\n;; from this boot's manifest) — the SA tier's bulk copies ARE qualified; this\n;; row is the jolt-array half.\n(joltffi-refuse-var! \"read-array\" \"byte-array moves need the jolt-array runtime, which this boot's manifest excludes\")\n(joltffi-refuse-var! \"read-into!\" \"byte-array moves need the jolt-array runtime, which this boot's manifest excludes\")\n(joltffi-refuse-var! \"write-array\" \"byte-array moves need the jolt-array runtime, which this boot's manifest excludes\")\n(joltffi-refuse-var! \"__read-array\" \"byte-array moves need the jolt-array runtime, which this boot's manifest excludes\")\n(joltffi-refuse-var! \"__write-array\" \"byte-array moves need the jolt-array runtime, which this boot's manifest excludes\")\n(joltffi-refuse-var! \"__read-into!\" \"byte-array moves need the jolt-array runtime, which this boot's manifest excludes\")\n(joltffi-refuse-var! \"__read-into-typed!\" \"typed-array views over foreign memory are not in the stage-1 registry\")\n(joltffi-refuse-var! \"__write-array-typed\" \"typed-array views over foreign memory are not in the stage-1 registry\")\n(joltffi-refuse-var! \"__byte-buffer\" \"direct ByteBuffers over foreign memory are not in the stage-1 registry\")\n(joltffi-refuse-var! \"free-callable\" \"foreign callables are unqualified (no arm64/x86_64 trampoline generator in stock Gambit 4.9.8)\")\n(joltffi-refuse-var! \"register-export\" \"foreign callables and AOT are unqualified on the gambit target\")\n(joltffi-refuse-var! \"__arena-callback\" \"foreign callables are unqualified (no arm64/x86_64 trampoline generator in stock Gambit 4.9.8)\")\n(joltffi-refuse-var! \"__auto-guard!\" \"auto arenas (collector-reclaimed) need guardians over foreign memory, unqualified in stage-1\")\n(joltffi-refuse-var! \"__auto-reclaimed\" \"auto arenas (collector-reclaimed) need guardians over foreign memory, unqualified in stage-1\")\n(joltffi-refuse-var! \"__gc-stall-reporter!\" \"the GC stall watch has no hook on this target (sa-gc-install-stall-watch! answers #f)\")\n(joltffi-refuse-var! \"stack-args-correct?\" \"the Chez #1091 stack-args probe is Chez-ABI-only and is not re-probed under the Gambit ABI in stage-1\")\n(joltffi-refuse-var! \"load-native\" \"superseded by load-library on this target (the adapter's scoped loader is the single dlopen path)\")\n\n;; ---- implemented rows -----------------------------------------------------------\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"load-library\" joltffi-load-library)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"loaded?\" joltffi-loaded?)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"load-system-library\" joltffi-load-system-library)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"system-library-candidates\"\n  (lambda (n) (make-pvec (list->vector (joltffi-system-library-candidates n)))))\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"load-failure-note\" joltffi-load-failure-note-var)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"find-symbol\" joltffi-find-symbol)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"dlsym-native\" jolt-ffi-dlsym-native)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"defining-libraries\" joltffi-defining-libraries)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"alloc\" joltffi-alloc)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"free\" joltffi-host-free)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"__alloc\" joltffi-alloc)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"__free\" joltffi-host-free)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"__calloc\" joltffi-calloc)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"read\" joltffi-read-fn)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"write\" joltffi-write-fn)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"__read\" joltffi-read-fn)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"__write\" joltffi-write-fn)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"copy\" joltffi-copy)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"__copy\" joltffi-copy)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"sizeof\" (lambda (ty) (joltffi-sizeof-chez (joltffi-type->chez ty))))\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"__sizeof\" (lambda (ty) (joltffi-sizeof-chez (joltffi-type->chez ty))))\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"__size\" joltffi-recorded-size)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"__remember-size!\" joltffi-remember-size!)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"__forget-sizes!\" joltffi-forget-sizes!)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"read-bytes\" joltffi-read-bytes)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"write-bytes\" joltffi-write-bytes)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"__read-bytes\" joltffi-read-bytes)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"__write-bytes\" joltffi-write-bytes)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"null?\" (lambda (p) (if (joltffi-null? p) #t #f)))\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"null\" joltffi-null)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"ptr->string\" joltffi-ptr->string)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"string->ptr\" joltffi-string->ptr)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"__string->ptr\" joltffi-string->ptr)\n(def-var! \"jolt.ffi\" \"__utf8-length\" joltffi-utf8-length)\n;; Ahead-of-time native bridge for the bounded Gambit FFI tier.\n;; generated-shims.scm supplies typed calls from shapes.json (including the\n;; variadic boundary). Blocking, callables, aggregates, runtime signatures and\n;; pinning remain refused in the adapter/compiler. See README.md for coverage.\n;;\n;; Capture cells are allocated per invocation before entering C. Each typed\n;; shim saves errno immediately after its target, before conversion or cleanup,\n;; then writes the caller-owned cell. No last-errno global/TLS cell is used.\n;; The test build injects a clobber AFTER that save; the wrong-order control\n;; saves AFTER its clobber. Neither control qualifies callbacks or blocking.\n;;\n;; Native pointers cross as exact integers through unsigned-long. Static\n;; assertions enforce LP64. Only the provisioned macOS arm64 leg is qualified.\n;; Bulk copies borrow a u8vector body for one non-allocating synchronous C call;\n;; they retain no Scheme address and are never collect-safe.\n\n(c-declare #<<JOLTFFI-C-BEGIN\n\n#include <errno.h>\n#include <stdint.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n#include <dlfcn.h>\n#include <stdarg.h>\n#include <sys/types.h>\n_Static_assert(sizeof(void*) == 8 && sizeof(long) == 8 && sizeof(int) == 4, \"stage-1 requires LP64\");\n\nstatic int joltffi_capture_test_enabled(void)\n{\n#ifdef JOLTFFI_CAPTURE_TEST\n  return 1;\n#else\n  return 0;\n#endif\n}\nstatic char *joltffi_c_os(void)\n{\n#ifdef __APPLE__\n  return \"darwin\";\n#else\n  return \"linux\";\n#endif\n}\n\n/* ---- loader flags (compile-time platform, as the Chez loader probes them) ---\n   RTLD_NOW on every POSIX host; RTLD_LOCAL on darwin is 0x4, on Linux 0 (the\n   default, matching Chez's ffi-rtld-flags table in host/chez/java/ffi.ss). */\n#define JOLTFFI_RTLD_NOW   2\n#ifdef __APPLE__\n#define JOLTFFI_RTLD_LOCAL 0x4\n#else\n#define JOLTFFI_RTLD_LOCAL 0\n#endif\n\n/* ---- loader ----------------------------------------------------------------- */\nstatic unsigned long joltffi_c_dlopen (const char *path)\n{ return ___CAST(unsigned long, dlopen (path, JOLTFFI_RTLD_NOW | JOLTFFI_RTLD_LOCAL)); }\n\nstatic unsigned long joltffi_c_dlsym (unsigned long h, const char *name)\n{ return ___CAST(unsigned long, dlsym (___CAST(void *, h), name)); }\n\n/* process-global resolution (RTLD_DEFAULT): the process's own symbols plus\n   RTLD_GLOBAL objects. RTLD_LOCAL loaded natives are deliberately NOT visible\n   here — the scoped-before-global isolation guarantee. */\nstatic unsigned long joltffi_c_dlsym_global (const char *name)\n{ return ___CAST(unsigned long, dlsym (RTLD_DEFAULT, name)); }\n\nstatic unsigned long joltffi_c_dlopen_global (const char *path)\n{ return ___CAST(unsigned long, dlopen (path, JOLTFFI_RTLD_NOW)); }   /* sa-load-shared-object of #f */\n\nstatic char *joltffi_c_dlerror (void)\n{ return ___CAST(char *, dlerror ()); }\n\nstatic int joltffi_c_dlclose (unsigned long h)\n{ return dlclose (___CAST(void *, h)); }\n\n/* ---- memory ----------------------------------------------------------------- */\nstatic unsigned long joltffi_c_malloc (unsigned long n)\n{ return ___CAST(unsigned long, malloc (___CAST(size_t, n))); }\n\nstatic void joltffi_c_free (unsigned long p)\n{ free (___CAST(void *, p)); }\n\nstatic void joltffi_c_zero (unsigned long p, unsigned long n)\n{ memset (___CAST(void *, p), 0, ___CAST(size_t, n)); }\n\n/* foreign -> bytevector. Returns 0 ok, -1 bad count. */\nstatic int joltffi_c_bytes_to_bv (___SCMOBJ bv, unsigned long src, unsigned long n)\n{\n  ___SCMOBJ ___temp; /* the subtype test macros need a temp cell */\n  if (___HAS_TAG_AND_IS_NOT_DOUBLE (bv, ___tSUBTYPED, ___TB) && ___TESTSUBTYPE (bv, ___sU8VECTOR))\n    {\n      size_t len = ___U8VECTORSIZE (bv);\n      if (___CAST(unsigned long, len) < n)\n        return -1;\n      if (n > 0)\n        memcpy (___BODY_AS (bv, ___tU8VECTOR), ___CAST(void *, src), ___CAST(size_t, n));\n      return 0;\n    }\n  return -1;\n}\n\n/* bytevector -> foreign. Returns 0 ok, -1 bad count. */\nstatic int joltffi_c_bytes_from_bv (unsigned long dst, ___SCMOBJ bv, unsigned long n)\n{\n  ___SCMOBJ ___temp; /* the subtype test macros need a temp cell */\n  if (___HAS_TAG_AND_IS_NOT_DOUBLE (bv, ___tSUBTYPED, ___TB) && ___TESTSUBTYPE (bv, ___sU8VECTOR))\n    {\n      size_t len = ___U8VECTORSIZE (bv);\n      if (___CAST(unsigned long, len) < n)\n        return -1;\n      if (n > 0)\n        memcpy (___CAST(void *, dst), ___BODY_AS (bv, ___tU8VECTOR), ___CAST(size_t, n));\n      return 0;\n    }\n  return -1;\n}\n\n/* read one int (4 bytes) from a caller-allocated errno cell */\nstatic int joltffi_c_read_i32 (unsigned long p)\n{ int v; memcpy (&v, ___CAST(void *, p), 4); return v; }\n\n/* Gate controls invoke the SAME errno-setting target, then clobber before or\n   after capture. Production capture shims also run the post-capture clobber\n   when built with JOLTFFI_CAPTURE_TEST; production builds omit that hook. */\nstatic int joltffi_c_ctrl_wrong_order(unsigned long fn, unsigned long out)\n{\n  int r = ((int (*)(int,int,int))fn)(-1,0,0);\n  errno = 4242;\n  *(int *)out = errno;\n  return r;\n}\nstatic int joltffi_c_preserve_control(unsigned long fn, unsigned long out)\n{\n  errno = 7777; /* preset inside this C invocation, before the target */\n  int r = ((int (*)(int,int,int))fn)(1,2,3);\n  int saved = errno;\n  *(int *)out = saved;\n  errno = 4242;\n  return r;\n}\n/* ---- typed data-plane access (sa-foreign-ref / sa-foreign-set!) -------------\n   memcpy-based so unaligned offsets behave; no errno semantics here — these are\n   plain readers, not call shims. Each returns through a caller-supplied\n   ___SCMOBJ only for the c-lambda wrapper below to keep ONE conversion shape;\n   the scalar wrappers return the value directly through the c-lambda result\n   conversion instead. */\n\nstatic signed char joltffi_c_ref_i8 (unsigned long p, unsigned long off)\n{ signed char v; memcpy (&v, ___CAST(void *, p + off), 1); return v; }\nstatic unsigned char joltffi_c_ref_u8 (unsigned long p, unsigned long off)\n{ unsigned char v; memcpy (&v, ___CAST(void *, p + off), 1); return v; }\nstatic short joltffi_c_ref_i16 (unsigned long p, unsigned long off)\n{ short v; memcpy (&v, ___CAST(void *, p + off), 2); return v; }\nstatic unsigned short joltffi_c_ref_u16 (unsigned long p, unsigned long off)\n{ unsigned short v; memcpy (&v, ___CAST(void *, p + off), 2); return v; }\nstatic int32_t joltffi_c_ref_i32 (unsigned long p, unsigned long off)\n{ int32_t v; memcpy (&v, ___CAST(void *, p + off), 4); return v; }\nstatic uint32_t joltffi_c_ref_u32 (unsigned long p, unsigned long off)\n{ uint32_t v; memcpy (&v, ___CAST(void *, p + off), 4); return v; }\nstatic int64_t joltffi_c_ref_i64 (unsigned long p, unsigned long off)\n{ int64_t v; memcpy (&v, ___CAST(void *, p + off), 8); return v; }\nstatic uint64_t joltffi_c_ref_u64 (unsigned long p, unsigned long off)\n{ uint64_t v; memcpy (&v, ___CAST(void *, p + off), 8); return v; }\nstatic float joltffi_c_ref_f32 (unsigned long p, unsigned long off)\n{ float v; memcpy (&v, ___CAST(void *, p + off), 4); return v; }\nstatic double joltffi_c_ref_f64 (unsigned long p, unsigned long off)\n{ double v; memcpy (&v, ___CAST(void *, p + off), 8); return v; }\n\nstatic void joltffi_c_set_i8 (unsigned long p, unsigned long off, signed char v)\n{ memcpy (___CAST(void *, p + off), &v, 1); }\nstatic void joltffi_c_set_u8 (unsigned long p, unsigned long off, unsigned char v)\n{ memcpy (___CAST(void *, p + off), &v, 1); }\nstatic void joltffi_c_set_i16 (unsigned long p, unsigned long off, short v)\n{ memcpy (___CAST(void *, p + off), &v, 2); }\nstatic void joltffi_c_set_u16 (unsigned long p, unsigned long off, unsigned short v)\n{ memcpy (___CAST(void *, p + off), &v, 2); }\nstatic void joltffi_c_set_i32 (unsigned long p, unsigned long off, int32_t v)\n{ memcpy (___CAST(void *, p + off), &v, 4); }\nstatic void joltffi_c_set_u32 (unsigned long p, unsigned long off, uint32_t v)\n{ memcpy (___CAST(void *, p + off), &v, 4); }\nstatic void joltffi_c_set_i64 (unsigned long p, unsigned long off, int64_t v)\n{ memcpy (___CAST(void *, p + off), &v, 8); }\nstatic void joltffi_c_set_u64 (unsigned long p, unsigned long off, uint64_t v)\n{ memcpy (___CAST(void *, p + off), &v, 8); }\nstatic void joltffi_c_set_f32 (unsigned long p, unsigned long off, float v)\n{ memcpy (___CAST(void *, p + off), &v, 4); }\nstatic void joltffi_c_set_f64 (unsigned long p, unsigned long off, double v)\n{ memcpy (___CAST(void *, p + off), &v, 8); }\n\nJOLTFFI-C-BEGIN\n\n)\n\n;; ---- Scheme wrappers ----------------------------------------------------------\n;; One per shim: the c-lambda IS the conversion boundary (arguments convert at\n;; the call; the C body runs with the converted values). Capture wrappers take\n;; the errout cell address between fn and the declared args; the adapter\n;; allocates and frees the cell around each call.\n\n(define (joltffi-capture-test-enabled?)\n  (= 1 ((c-lambda () int \"joltffi_capture_test_enabled\"))))\n(define (joltffi-platform-key)\n  ((c-lambda () char-string \"joltffi_c_os\")))\n\n;; ---- loader wrappers ----------------------------------------------------------\n(define (joltffi-dlopen path)\n  ((c-lambda (char-string) unsigned-long \"joltffi_c_dlopen\") path))\n(define (joltffi-dlopen-global path)\n  ((c-lambda (char-string) unsigned-long \"joltffi_c_dlopen_global\") path))\n(define (joltffi-dlsym handle name)\n  ((c-lambda (unsigned-long char-string) unsigned-long \"joltffi_c_dlsym\") handle name))\n(define (joltffi-dlsym-global name)\n  ((c-lambda (char-string) unsigned-long \"joltffi_c_dlsym_global\") name))\n(define (joltffi-dlerror)\n  ((c-lambda () char-string \"joltffi_c_dlerror\")))\n(define (joltffi-dlclose handle)\n  ((c-lambda (unsigned-long) int \"joltffi_c_dlclose\") handle))\n\n;; ---- memory wrappers ----------------------------------------------------------\n(define (joltffi-malloc n)\n  ((c-lambda (unsigned-long) unsigned-long \"joltffi_c_malloc\") n))\n(define (joltffi-free p)\n  ((c-lambda (unsigned-long) void \"joltffi_c_free\") p))\n(define (joltffi-zero p n)\n  ((c-lambda (unsigned-long unsigned-long) void \"joltffi_c_zero\") p n))\n;; 0 ok / -1 count-too-large: the adapter raises before dereferencing anything.\n(define (joltffi-bytes-to-bv! bv src n)\n  ((c-lambda (scheme-object unsigned-long unsigned-long) int \"joltffi_c_bytes_to_bv\") bv src n))\n(define (joltffi-bytes-from-bv! dst bv n)\n  ((c-lambda (unsigned-long scheme-object unsigned-long) int \"joltffi_c_bytes_from_bv\") dst bv n))\n(define (joltffi-read-i32 p)\n  ((c-lambda (unsigned-long) int \"joltffi_c_read_i32\") p))\n\n;; ---- the negative-control entry (gate-only; see the C comment above) ----------\n(define (joltffi-ctrl-wrong-order fn errout)\n  ((c-lambda (unsigned-long unsigned-long) int \"joltffi_c_ctrl_wrong_order\") fn errout))\n\n\n(define (joltffi-preserve-control fn out)\n  ((c-lambda (unsigned-long unsigned-long) int \"joltffi_c_preserve_control\") fn out))\n\n;; ---- typed data-plane wrappers (sa-foreign-ref / sa-foreign-set!) ------------\n(define (joltffi-ref-i8 addr off) ((c-lambda (unsigned-long unsigned-long) int8 \"joltffi_c_ref_i8\") addr off))\n(define (joltffi-ref-u8 addr off) ((c-lambda (unsigned-long unsigned-long) unsigned-int8 \"joltffi_c_ref_u8\") addr off))\n(define (joltffi-ref-i16 addr off) ((c-lambda (unsigned-long unsigned-long) int16 \"joltffi_c_ref_i16\") addr off))\n(define (joltffi-ref-u16 addr off) ((c-lambda (unsigned-long unsigned-long) unsigned-int16 \"joltffi_c_ref_u16\") addr off))\n(define (joltffi-ref-i32 addr off) ((c-lambda (unsigned-long unsigned-long) int32 \"joltffi_c_ref_i32\") addr off))\n(define (joltffi-ref-u32 addr off) ((c-lambda (unsigned-long unsigned-long) unsigned-int32 \"joltffi_c_ref_u32\") addr off))\n(define (joltffi-ref-i64 addr off) ((c-lambda (unsigned-long unsigned-long) int64 \"joltffi_c_ref_i64\") addr off))\n(define (joltffi-ref-u64 addr off) ((c-lambda (unsigned-long unsigned-long) unsigned-int64 \"joltffi_c_ref_u64\") addr off))\n(define (joltffi-ref-f32 addr off) ((c-lambda (unsigned-long unsigned-long) float32 \"joltffi_c_ref_f32\") addr off))\n(define (joltffi-ref-f64 addr off) ((c-lambda (unsigned-long unsigned-long) float64 \"joltffi_c_ref_f64\") addr off))\n(define (joltffi-set-i8! addr off v) ((c-lambda (unsigned-long unsigned-long int8) void \"joltffi_c_set_i8\") addr off v))\n(define (joltffi-set-u8! addr off v) ((c-lambda (unsigned-long unsigned-long unsigned-int8) void \"joltffi_c_set_u8\") addr off v))\n(define (joltffi-set-i16! addr off v) ((c-lambda (unsigned-long unsigned-long int16) void \"joltffi_c_set_i16\") addr off v))\n(define (joltffi-set-u16! addr off v) ((c-lambda (unsigned-long unsigned-long unsigned-int16) void \"joltffi_c_set_u16\") addr off v))\n(define (joltffi-set-i32! addr off v) ((c-lambda (unsigned-long unsigned-long int32) void \"joltffi_c_set_i32\") addr off v))\n(define (joltffi-set-u32! addr off v) ((c-lambda (unsigned-long unsigned-long unsigned-int32) void \"joltffi_c_set_u32\") addr off v))\n(define (joltffi-set-i64! addr off v) ((c-lambda (unsigned-long unsigned-long int64) void \"joltffi_c_set_i64\") addr off v))\n(define (joltffi-set-u64! addr off v) ((c-lambda (unsigned-long unsigned-long unsigned-int64) void \"joltffi_c_set_u64\") addr off v))\n(define (joltffi-set-f32! addr off v) ((c-lambda (unsigned-long unsigned-long float32) void \"joltffi_c_set_f32\") addr off v))\n(define (joltffi-set-f64! addr off v) ((c-lambda (unsigned-long unsigned-long float64) void \"joltffi_c_set_f64\") addr off v))\n\n(##include \"generated-shims.scm\")\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat AI_POLICY.md; cat host/gambit/ffi/README.md; ls p1b-stage1; ls -la vendor'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '700,855p' host/chez/collections.ss; sed -n '1700,1800p' stdlib/jolt/ffi.clj; rg -n 'defcfn|load-source|booted|jolt\\\\.ffi' host/gambit/{*.ss,*.py,yin}\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat AI_POLICY.md; cat host/gambit/ffi/README.md; ls p1b-stage1; ls -la vendor'","aggregated_output":"## Stance on LLM Use in an Jolt\n\nThere is a view that code written by hand must be more intentional, almost by definition, since it requires the maintainer to put it there personally. That is, of course, true, but once a project grows past a certain size or has multiple maintainers, no one really has the totality of the code in their heads. Any new code that is added is therefore always done with limited understanding. Code being written by hand should not be equated with faithfully expressing intent. If that were the case, we would never have software bugs. Humans make mistakes all the time, as is clearly evidenced by the abundance of buggy code that predates LLM use.\n\nIt is simply not feasible for humans to keep the entirety of large projects in their heads all at once. When working on a project, one is constantly forgetting and relearning code along the way. The situation is even worse for projects where multiple people work together and no one knows what everyone else was thinking. We look at the code and try to build up sufficient context in our heads to make the necessary changes. When we misjudge that context or misunderstand existing code, we end up making mistakes.\n\nThe reality is that neither code produced by a human nor code produced by an LLM can be trusted to be free of defects. The way we ensure software quality is through a solid development process. The focus has to be on ensuring the quality of the code through code reviews, specifications, testing, and other well established practices. All of these same tools work just as well with LLM-generated code as they do with code written by hand.\n\nThe core skill in using these tools is the same as the skill needed when writing code manually. One has to understand the given requirement, the shape of the solution, and how to verify that the code produced actually matches the intent.\n\nWhat these tools do is automate the mechanical aspect of producing the code. Once it is written, you can read it, understand it, and change it as you would with any other code. The intention and the ideas come from the human using the tool, and new ways to automate the work of typing out code do not change the role or the responsibility of the developer.\n\nFocusing on how the code was produced rather than on the code itself dilutes the responsibility since blame should never be deflected to LLMs. The ownership of the code rests with the person submitting the code, and they must take responsibility for its quality and correctness. Either the code is clean and lives up to the standards of the project, or it will be rejected and the author will be asked to make changes to address the problems.\n# Gambit native FFI: stage 1 (partial)\n\nThis tier implements native memory, scoped loading, registered ordinary calls,\ninvocation-local errno capture, and real `##gc`. It is qualified here only on\n**macOS arm64, the provisioned single-threaded Gambit 4.9.8**. P2/P3 consumers\nremain Chez-only. It does not provide GC-safe blocking or callbacks.\n\nRun from the checkout root:\n\n```sh\nGAMBIT_PREFIX=/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/gambit/4.9.8\nsh host/gambit/ffi/record-toolchain.sh \"$GAMBIT_PREFIX\"\nmake gambitffi gambitcheck gambitkernel gambiteval GAMBIT_PREFIX=\"$GAMBIT_PREFIX\"\nmake gambitseedcheck CHEZ=/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/chezscheme/10.4.1/bin/scheme\n```\n\nAll new FFI gates fail if their toolchain or bridge is absent. `gambitcheck`\nrequires the bridge and runs both its adapter checks and the public-source\nwitness. The ABI target also runs that public witness. Every native artifact\nis rebuilt on each make invocation, after input and toolchain digest checks.\nA timed-out gate kills its process group and returns 124. `gambitffi` also\nrequires `emittedheads`, which checks emitted names against user-local shadowing.\n\n## Build and ABI identity\n\n`shapes.json` is the reviewed stage-1 projection of the 44-shape inventory\n`p1b-inv/shim-registry.edn` at fork `a6852ef8`. It includes ordinary procedure\nshapes S01/S01b/S02/S03/S12/S12b/S13/S18/S19/S20/S21/S22/S22b/S22c/S23/\nS26/S26b/S26c/S26d/S26e, the S32 declared-tail fixture (two fixed arguments),\nand thirteen explicit scalar identity fixtures. S19 and S20 share one ABI.\nBlocking/callable/pointer-call/aggregate/bare-varargs shapes are refused.\nOther shapes are deliberately rejected; this is not unrestricted FFI.\n\n`generate-shims.py` emits `generated-shims.scm` from that manifest. Identity\nincludes argument types, result type, capture mode, and **variadic boundary**.\nFixed `(int,int,int)` and variadic `(int,int,...)` must use different C types\non Apple arm64. Chez `integer-32` tokens map to Gambit `int32` conversions.\nPointers cross as exact integers; static assertions require LP64.\n\nAfter an intentional input change:\n\n```sh\npython3 host/gambit/ffi/generate-shims.py\nsh host/gambit/ffi/make-digests.sh\n```\n\nThe committed `DIGESTS.txt` pins the bridge sources, generated inputs and\nprovenance helper scripts. Verification always checks those digests and the\ngenerated-shim consistency first, even if toolchain provenance is missing.\n\nToolchain provenance belongs to the current checkout's installation:\n`build/gambit-ffi/provenance/TOOLCHAIN.<platform>.json` (ignored by Git).\nIt records the platform, resolved installation path, full `gsi -v` string,\nconfigure-summary SHA256, and SHA256 of gsi/gsc, gambuild-C, gambit.h and the\nthree runtime/compiler libraries. The configure summary is the final quoted\n`./configure ...` field reported by Gambit 4.9.8's `gsi -v`, hashed as UTF-8\nwithout its surrounding double quotes or a final newline. Unsupported version\nor summary formats refuse. JSON keys are sorted; no recording timestamp is\nadded. Re-recording an unchanged install produces identical bytes.\n\nThe key is lowercase OS/architecture from `os.uname()`, with `aarch64` mapped\nto `arm64` and `amd64` to `x86_64`. Darwin/Linux on arm64/x86_64 are accepted;\nother OS/architectures refuse explicitly. Android/bionic's Linux/aarch64 uname\nselects `linux-arm64`; it needs its own local installation record, without a\nprecommitted platform file. This selector support does not qualify Android\nnative execution. The current Termux CI job installs Chez only and retains\nthe adapter gates' no-Gambit skips.\n\nAfter provisioning or intentionally replacing an installation, record it once\nfrom the checkout root:\n\n```sh\nsh host/gambit/ffi/watchdog.sh 60 sh host/gambit/ffi/record-toolchain.sh \"$GAMBIT_PREFIX\"\nmake gambitffi-verify-digests GAMBIT_PREFIX=\"$GAMBIT_PREFIX\"\n```\n\nFor the orchestrator's Linux clone, set\n`GAMBIT_PREFIX=\"$HOME/.local/share/mise/installs/gambit/4.9.8\"` first.\nThe recorder prints, for example:\n\n```text\ngambitffi: platform=linux-x86_64 manifest=<checkout>/build/gambit-ffi/provenance/TOOLCHAIN.linux-x86_64.json\ngambitffi: recorded local toolchain provenance (7 inputs); source digests unchanged\n```\n\nVerification prints the computed platform and same manifest, then\n`gambitffi: local toolchain provenance verified (7 inputs)`.\nMissing, malformed or stale records refuse with the exact record command.\nVerification never records or refreshes provenance, and recording never\nrewrites the committed source digest index. Binary hashes are compared only\nwith that install's local record, allowing independently built CI installs.\nThe tests workflow records its own fresh/cached /opt/gambit install immediately\nafter setup, before the gates. Keep local records and build outputs uncommitted.\n\nRecording provenance does not qualify Linux execution, ABI or consumer parity.\nAll four fixture link commands select `-dynamiclib` on Darwin and\n`-shared -fPIC` on Linux; other OS names refuse. The existing `.dylib` fixture\nfilenames are retained on both hosts. Linux link-plan checks on macOS are not\nexecution or ABI qualification on Linux.\n\nThe ABI gate gets its errno-accessor name from that same Makefile OS selector:\n`__error` on Darwin and `__errno_location` on glibc Linux. Both functions return\n`int *`; both require one int dereference. See the\n[Apple header](https://github.com/apple-oss-distributions/xnu/blob/main/bsd/sys/errno.h)\nand [glibc header](https://github.com/bminor/glibc/blob/master/stdlib/errno.h).\nThe fixture verifies the selected address equals `&errno`, sets errno to 7777,\nand reads the int immediately in one C invocation, avoiding ambient changes\nbetween Scheme calls. Invoke the ABI gate through `make gambitffi-abi`, which\nsupplies `JOLT_FFI_ERRNO_ACCESSOR`; missing/unsupported selections refuse.\n\nThe helper's acceptance, deterministic-recording and negative checks can be run\nwith `python3 host/gambit/ffi/test-toolchain.py` (set TMPDIR inside the checkout\nto retain the task's scratch-file boundary). Its Linux metadata/files are\nsynthetic fixtures, not execution on a Linux host.\n\n`build/gambit-ffi/` contains output SHA256 records, compiler version, runtime\nhashes and retained generated C. Binary digests are recorded, not claimed\nidentical across machines/linkers. No runtime sources or dependencies are\nmodified or downloaded.\n\n## Public path and limits\n\n`gambitffi-public.ss` boots the fork's actual `host/gambit/boot.ss` and reminted\ncompiler seed, asserts `(jolt.backend-scheme.session/target) => :gambit`, then\nruns jolt source through `jolt-compile-eval`. It covers host-owned `jolt.ffi`\nmemory/loader functions and the public compiler's low-level `__cfn` form:\nordinary, declared-tail variadic, nullable string, float, bool, exact-width and\nerrno-pair calls. It checks catchable layout, named-binding, blocking+errno,\naggregate, callable and bare-tail refusals, plus loader diagnostics.\n\nGambit eval cannot see unit-local macros. Emitted code therefore calls the\nhost's `joltffi-public-procedure`, which invokes the adapter's finite registry\nconstructors with literal type data and translates native errors into\nmessage-carrying jolt exceptions. Adapter `sa-foreign-*` syntaxes remain\navailable to same-unit Scheme callers. No `c-lambda` is evaluated at runtime.\nThe `:gambit` primitive table explicitly selects the empty unsafe-op prefix.\n\nThis is the fork's compiler/eval gate path, not `bin/jolt build`: the build CLI,\nAOT/FASL/subprocess, module/native dependency loading and complete stdlib\n`jolt.ffi` namespace loading are not qualified on Gambit. Explicit library\nloading works; pending `:jolt/native` discovery is not implemented. Public\njolt-array copy helpers remain refused because this boot excludes their\nruntime; adapter u8vector bulk copies and public string-byte copies work.\nString arguments remain refused; native copies via `string->ptr` work.\nNo Web/JS, Linux, MT, or native consumer parity is claimed.\n\nThe real collector witness clears an unreachable weak key. It does not test\ncollection during C execution. Bulk copies borrow Scheme storage only for a\nshort non-allocating synchronous call and retain no borrowed address.\nObject pinning is refused.\n\n## Errno proof\n\nEvery generated capture shim executes target call, immediate `saved = errno`,\nthen writes a caller-owned cell, before Gambit conversion or cleanup. The\nadapter allocates one cell before the call and frees it with `dynamic-wind`,\nincluding conversion failure; ordered `let*` reads only after the wrapper.\nThere is no shared last-errno slot.\n\nThe errno target builds the same generator output with\n`JOLTFFI_CAPTURE_TEST`: every capture body clobbers errno *after* saving it.\nThe gate asserts that instrumentation is enabled. A deliberately wrong control\ncalls the same failing target and saves *after* an identical clobber; it must\nobserve 4242, while the actual shim must observe the target's EACCES. Success\nwith an errno-setting target, a preset-and-preserving C-frame control,\npost-capture clobber, and 1000 alternating result pairs are checked too.\nAlternating calls are stress coverage, not proof of native concurrency,\nreentrant callbacks, or blocking-plus-errno.\n\n## Inventory questions\n\n- Q1/Q2: layout and by-value emission refuse before Chez ftype forms; named\n  bindings use the registry, preserve variadic identity, and refuse with a\n  message for unknown signatures. Public tests prevent unbound-name failures.\n- Q3: parser worker destruction's unmarked join remains a consumer issue for\n  P3. S18 only qualifies the ABI with a nonblocking fixture, not that consumer.\n  Do not enable parser workers on this evidence; re-mark or separately qualify\n  the wait before P3 adoption.\n- Q4: explicit `:gambit {:unsafe-prefix \"\"}` entry implemented and reminted.\n- Q5: both callable syntaxes added to CONTRACT.txt; refusal assertions retained.\n- Q6: unmarked SSL waits remain unqualified; no libssl binding/consumer parity\n  is established by ordinary-call shape support. Address marks in its consumer\n  lane before adoption.\n\nRollback is a revert of the stage-1 commits, followed by removing the generated\n`build/gambit-ffi/` directory. No installed Gambit runtime is changed.\n1795042000000-engineer-p1b-stage1-codex-gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md\nP1b-ci-r2.findings.md\nP1b-ci.findings.md\nP1b-linux-provenance.findings.md\nevidence\ntotal 0\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 10 sto  staff   320 Nov 22 00:10 .\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 35 sto  staff  1120 Nov 22 00:10 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x@  2 sto  staff    64 Nov 22 00:10 cli\ndrwxr-xr-x@  2 sto  staff    64 Nov 22 00:10 clojure-test-suite\ndrwxr-xr-x@  2 sto  staff    64 Nov 22 00:10 fs\ndrwxr-xr-x@  2 sto  staff    64 Nov 22 00:10 grenadine\ndrwxr-xr-x@  5 sto  staff   160 Nov 22 00:10 grenadine-generated\ndrwxr-xr-x@  2 sto  staff    64 Nov 22 00:10 irregex\ndrwxr-xr-x@  2 sto  staff    64 Nov 22 00:10 process\ndrwxr-xr-x@  2 sto  staff    64 Nov 22 00:10 sci\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
